          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Core unit inquiry",
          "content": "> **Book 18 / undergraduate editorial draft**. All numerical cases in this book are invented for classroom use. Independent academic review is pending.\n\n**Question:** What does a scientifically justified claim of conservation importance or urgency require?\n\n**Bounded setting:** species identification, ecological composition and the boundaries of assessment. **Fictional running setting:** a fictional river delta undergoing habitat conversion. The five lessons share the conceptual aim of following an ecological claim from biological mechanism to measurement, inference and a justifiable decision. Do not confuse legal designation, species-richness totals or unverified threat labels with demonstrated persistence. The emphasis is neither pessimism nor optimism; it is disciplined reasoning about actions that influence living systems over time.\n\nConservation starts with a definition of what is being protected: one named species, a population process, a habitat mosaic or a set of interactions. The chosen objective changes the evidence needed. A record of many generalist species may indicate richness but not ecological continuity, while a small reserve containing one threatened habitat can be disproportionately significant. Examine the boundary of each biological claim: taxonomic accuracy, spatial domain, interval of observation and whether the criterion describes a stock, a pressure or a response. A threat assessment is credible only when it documents the relevant driver and distinguishes what is measured from what is presumed.\n\nThe conservation-priority problem illustrates a normative layer. Should resources focus on the species with highest extinction probability, the action with the greatest expected response, the most irreplaceable landscape or the livelihoods of those bearing restrictions? There is no purely biological calculation that makes rights or equity disappear. Scientific evidence informs the consequences, while transparent governance sets legitimate objectives and constraints. In the lessons below, note how exactly the same inventory can support different decisions under different aims.\n\nBefore starting, create a two-column notebook. In the first column write statements that directly follow from a defined observation; in the second place the wider interpretation that needs extra assumptions. At the end of the unit, record which observation would most change your preferred conservation decision. This exercise is deliberately not a ranking of students' values. Its purpose is to make the relationship between observations, inference and public choices explicit.",
