          "secNumber": "1.2",
          "heading": "Lesson 1.1 \u2014 Dimensions of biodiversity",
          "content": "**Guiding question.** How can we distinguish a defensible finding about **dimensions of biodiversity** from a pattern that could arise through an alternative mechanism or a biased sample? The aim is to explain the ecological idea, define an appropriate quantity, reproduce an elementary numerical example, interrogate a counterargument, and connect the evidence to a legitimate practical decision.\n\n### The ecological mechanism and what it predicts\n\nGenetic variation, species identities, community composition, functional traits and ecosystem processes represent different dimensions; a higher species count need not indicate greater phylogenetic breadth or stronger ecological function. For this mechanism, identify the biological scale and time interval. Does an observation concern one organism, a local population or regional persistence? Name one observation that could contradict the explanation and distinguish the measured state from its inferred cause.\n\n### A field measurement contract\n\nRecord taxonomic richness together with trait distributions, abundance, sampling coverage and within-species variation across comparable plots; justify the indicator with the management question. Define the sampling unit, eligibility, survey timing and denominators in advance. Keep auditable observations and appropriately restricted provenance so independent analysts can identify sources of bias.\n\n**Methodological lens \u2014 comparison across space.** Select at least three independent locations within each management class, describe how sites were chosen and report environmental covariates. Differences between sites may precede the management action; avoid claiming intervention effects from one-time cross-sectional samples. Document how site choice or altered detection could change the measured result.\n\n### Synthetic ecological case and competing interpretation\n\nTwo wetlands each contain 20 named species, yet the first is dominated by tolerant generalists while the second supports rare specialists and functionally diverse feeding groups. This is a fictional case, not a measured local trend. Identify response, suspected driver and an unmeasured common cause. Would the conclusion change if the monitoring method became more sensitive?\n\n**Main interpretive trap.** Richness alone erases turnover, dominance, identity and ecological roles; an inventory can also inflate records by changing identification effort. Name a comparison site and independent indicator that could expose this error. Specify the result that would force revision of the preferred explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative case: Species richness across comparable plots\n\n**Assumed invented input.** Nineteen observed taxa in a 1-ha plot against fifteen in a comparably sampled plot; no inference on functional diversity.\n\n**Expression:** `(19-15)/15*100`. **Calculated answer:** **26.667 % change**.\n\nIdentify the units and denominator. This arithmetic alone supplies no uncertainty interval or causal evidence. Change a central input by 20%; report whether the management interpretation changes and why.\n\n### Evidence appraisal and practical choice\n\n**Field reasoning.** Represent the causal pathway as a set of stages: ecological source condition, exposure or interaction, demographic or community response, observed indicator and management interpretation. Describe which stage the proposed evidence directly addresses. A value measured at the end of a chain is not automatically a direct measurement of its cause. Record time delays between pressure and response; persistent adults, stored propagules and transient habitat use can postpone visible change. Apply this lens to **dimensions of biodiversity**: identify the actual observation, the larger unsupported inference and the smallest useful independent check. Explain how new evidence might change the next action.\n\n**Comparator selection.** Choose untreated or reference sites using ecological similarity rather than ease of access. A valid comparator can differ from a desired restoration state but must represent a plausible alternative trajectory. Prioritize indicators linked to ecological integrity and report uncertainty before declaring one site more valuable. A decision brief must still specify feasible alternatives, baseline and rights-related constraints; no quantitative success probability is implied.\n\n### Independent application exercise\n\n1. Sketch a causal graph of this case, naming a confounder and an observation that could disprove the leading explanation.\n2. Improve the survey design: specify sampling units, detection controls, an appropriate comparison and a revision trigger.\n3. Repeat the calculation with a 20% adverse change in one input, then draft a brief memo separating measured facts from management judgments.\n\n**Lesson takeaway.** Trace the scientific mechanism, observed indicator and authorized decision separately; no synthetic model is a forecast of a real site.",
