          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 1.5 \u2014 Conservation priorities and opportunity cost",
          "content": "**Guiding question.** How can we distinguish a defensible finding about **conservation priorities and opportunity cost** from a pattern that could arise through an alternative mechanism or a biased sample? The aim is to explain the ecological idea, define an appropriate quantity, reproduce an elementary numerical example, interrogate a counterargument, and connect the evidence to a legitimate practical decision.\n\n### The ecological mechanism and what it predicts\n\nScarce budgets require explicit objectives, costs, likelihood of success, reversibility, complementarity and distributional consequences; a single flagship species is rarely the only legitimate objective. For this mechanism, identify the biological scale and time interval. Does an observation concern one organism, a local population or regional persistence? Name one observation that could contradict the explanation and distinguish the measured state from its inferred cause.\n\n### A field measurement contract\n\nCompare candidate actions under consistent outcomes and realistic budgets, state the weights applied and inspect which rankings change under alternative values. Define the sampling unit, eligibility, survey timing and denominators in advance. Keep auditable observations and appropriately restricted provenance so independent analysts can identify sources of bias.\n\n**Methodological lens \u2014 decision-focused study.** Specify the decision that will change if a result is high or low, then choose the least complex credible measurement. Collecting more data is not always useful if the result cannot change an authorized decision. Document how site choice or altered detection could change the measured result.\n\n### Synthetic ecological case and competing interpretation\n\nA proposed reserve has high species richness but insecure long-term governance; another offers modest gains and strong local stewardship. This is a fictional case, not a measured local trend. Identify response, suspected driver and an unmeasured common cause. Would the conclusion change if the monitoring method became more sensitive?\n\n**Main interpretive trap.** An attractive numerical score can conceal value judgments, missing rights, ecological thresholds or uncertain implementation costs. Name a comparison site and independent indicator that could expose this error. Specify the result that would force revision of the preferred explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative case: Expected gain per budget unit\n\n**Assumed invented input.** An invented intervention promises 40 outcome units with 0.75 success probability at cost 10 funding units.\n\n**Expression:** `(40*.75)/10`. **Calculated answer:** **3 outcome units per funding unit**.\n\nIdentify the units and denominator. This arithmetic alone supplies no uncertainty interval or causal evidence. Change a central input by 20%; report whether the management interpretation changes and why.\n\n### Evidence appraisal and practical choice\n\n**Scale and transfer.** An effect observed in one patch does not automatically apply to a landscape, and a ten-year forecast is not validated by a two-month trial. Consider spatial heterogeneity, ecological feedbacks, temporal autocorrelation and the potential displacement of pressures. When extending a conclusion, explicitly identify which biological processes are plausibly stable and which depend on new conditions. Poor transportability often matters more than precision within the original sample. Apply this lens to **conservation priorities and opportunity cost**: identify the actual observation, the larger unsupported inference and the smallest useful independent check. Explain how new evidence might change the next action.\n\n**Stakeholder assessment.** Identify who gains, who bears costs, who controls access and who can challenge a decision. Good biodiversity statistics do not supply consent or equitable governance. Publish decision assumptions and alternatives; avoid presenting one formula as ethically neutral. A decision brief must still specify feasible alternatives, baseline and rights-related constraints; no quantitative success probability is implied.\n\n### Independent application exercise\n\n1. Sketch a causal graph of this case, naming a confounder and an observation that could disprove the leading explanation.\n2. Improve the survey design: specify sampling units, detection controls, an appropriate comparison and a revision trigger.\n3. Repeat the calculation with a 20% adverse change in one input, then draft a brief memo separating measured facts from management judgments.\n\n**Lesson takeaway.** Trace the scientific mechanism, observed indicator and authorized decision separately; no synthetic model is a forecast of a real site.",
