          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Integrated unit investigation \u2014 Estuary biodiversity priority portfolio",
          "content": "**Research and management setting.** Five habitats include tidal marsh, mudflat, mangrove edge, freshwater pools and grassland fringe. Compare recorded specialist diversity, area and local dependency. A high-richness habitat should not automatically outvote one containing irreplaceable specialist assemblages. This dataset is fictional and is not sourced from an actual site. Begin by specifying the intended population and the decision that requires evidence. A useful research question should distinguish a state variable, such as breeding occupancy or native habitat condition, from management inputs, such as field labor or expenditure. Decide which indicator could in principle move because the proposed intervention changed an ecological mechanism rather than because the observation process was altered.\n\n**Classroom data.** Synthetically assign habitat areas [60,25,80,15,40] hectares and field-observed specialist taxa [6,8,4,5,3]. Put each synthetic observation into a small table with a column for its unit, survey date or period, uncertainty and collection method. If an input is a judgment or a scenario assumption rather than an observation, label it as such. Draw a diagram showing how each measurement relates to the ecological process. Check dimensions before constructing composite indices: an area in hectares and a conservation score without units cannot be added without an explicit decision rule.\n\n**Analysis assignment.** Create a decision matrix with different weights on ecological irreplaceability, management feasibility and affected community priorities; report how the chosen action changes. For each alternative, state what is observed, what is assumed and what would require external validation. Give a best-case and a downside scenario, but do not attach unsupported probabilities. Explain which changes in sampling design or stakeholder priorities would change the conclusion. Illustrate your results with a chart that shows raw points alongside summary values; avoid hiding sample size in a visually smooth curve.\n\n**Critical evaluation.** State which indicators would need independent botanical or faunal identification before a real planning decision. A responsible report names at least two plausible alternative explanations. Propose a stronger monitoring design with a baseline or counterfactual, a biological outcome metric, a record of implementation and a realistic revision trigger. If the intervention entails handling wildlife, exposing locations or changing access, identify independent specialist review, legal permissions and affected decision makers; the invented project grants none of those permissions.\n\n**Submission format.** Produce (1) a one-page causal map, (2) a reproducible calculation sheet, (3) a table separating raw observations from modeled outputs, (4) a comparison of intervention and nonintervention alternatives, and (5) a short decision memo stating uncertainty and who can authorize further work. Peer reviewers should be able to find a changed assumption and recompute the conclusion without modifying undocumented spreadsheet cells. Respond in writing to one challenge from a skeptical reviewer rather than simply repeating the original recommendation.",
