          "secNumber": "2.1",
          "heading": "Core unit inquiry",
          "content": "> **Book 18 / undergraduate editorial draft**. All numerical cases in this book are invented for classroom use. Independent academic review is pending.\n\n**Question:** What combination of demography, genetics and dispersal predicts whether populations can persist?\n\n**Bounded setting:** time-indexed animal or plant populations linked by birth, death and movement. **Fictional running setting:** a hypothetical isolated grassland mammal metapopulation. The five lessons share the conceptual aim of following an ecological claim from biological mechanism to measurement, inference and a justifiable decision. The same population count can accompany very different recruitment, survival, effective size and future trajectories. The emphasis is neither pessimism nor optimism; it is disciplined reasoning about actions that influence living systems over time.\n\nA population is an evolving process rather than a number on a clipboard. Even a perfectly counted census can conceal a loss of breeding adults, recruitment failure or demographic imbalance. Stage-specific life cycles connect survival and fecundity to growth; genetics and movement change vulnerability in ways not captured by one rate. The risk can be gradual, such as persistently lower recruitment, or abrupt, such as a rare drought that simultaneously affects many patches. An elementary growth equation can clarify direction but does not determine the distribution of future outcomes.\n\nEvaluate models at the scale where they are used. Patch persistence matters for a meadow network; local recruitment matters for a breeding colony; effective size matters for loss of genetic variation over generations. If dispersal increases, demographic rescue may improve, but pathogens, synchronous disturbances or unsuitable destination habitat may change the result. The following lessons ask students to connect parameters to field methods and to identify why precise-looking forecasts can be fragile.\n\nBefore starting, create a two-column notebook. In the first column write statements that directly follow from a defined observation; in the second place the wider interpretation that needs extra assumptions. At the end of the unit, record which observation would most change your preferred conservation decision. This exercise is deliberately not a ranking of students' values. Its purpose is to make the relationship between observations, inference and public choices explicit.",
