          "secNumber": "2.2",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.1 \u2014 Life tables and vital rates",
          "content": "**Guiding question.** How can we distinguish a defensible finding about **life tables and vital rates** from a pattern that could arise through an alternative mechanism or a biased sample? The aim is to explain the ecological idea, define an appropriate quantity, reproduce an elementary numerical example, interrogate a counterargument, and connect the evidence to a legitimate practical decision.\n\n### The ecological mechanism and what it predicts\n\nPopulation trajectories depend on stage-specific survival, fecundity, movement and changing environments, not solely on adult census totals; age structure can produce transient increases. For this mechanism, identify the biological scale and time interval. Does an observation concern one organism, a local population or regional persistence? Name one observation that could contradict the explanation and distinguish the measured state from its inferred cause.\n\n### A field measurement contract\n\nEstimate juvenile, subadult and adult survival with consistent intervals, plus reproductive output and movement among sampled areas. Define the sampling unit, eligibility, survey timing and denominators in advance. Keep auditable observations and appropriately restricted provenance so independent analysts can identify sources of bias.\n\n**Methodological lens \u2014 comparison across space.** Select at least three independent locations within each management class, describe how sites were chosen and report environmental covariates. Differences between sites may precede the management action; avoid claiming intervention effects from one-time cross-sectional samples. Document how site choice or altered detection could change the measured result.\n\n### Synthetic ecological case and competing interpretation\n\nA deer population increases in adult count while juvenile survival and recruitment decline after drought in a fabricated example. This is a fictional case, not a measured local trend. Identify response, suspected driver and an unmeasured common cause. Would the conclusion change if the monitoring method became more sensitive?\n\n**Main interpretive trap.** Dividing total counts between years confounds births, deaths, immigration and detection changes. Name a comparison site and independent indicator that could expose this error. Specify the result that would force revision of the preferred explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative case: Annual survival proportion\n\n**Assumed invented input.** Sixty-three individuals recounted from seventy-five tagged animals, with emigration not distinguished from death.\n\n**Expression:** `63/75*100`. **Calculated answer:** **84 % apparent survival**.\n\nIdentify the units and denominator. This arithmetic alone supplies no uncertainty interval or causal evidence. Change a central input by 20%; report whether the management interpretation changes and why.\n\n### Evidence appraisal and practical choice\n\n**Decision quality.** An actionable claim should identify a decision maker, a feasible alternative, an indicator that responds on the appropriate time scale and a rule for revisiting the choice. When both options could harm affected people, ecological urgency does not remove the obligation to identify rights and safeguards. Note whether the action prevents a pressure, repairs a process, protects remaining habitat or merely improves knowledge. The intended outcome must match that pathway. Apply this lens to **life tables and vital rates**: identify the actual observation, the larger unsupported inference and the smallest useful independent check. Explain how new evidence might change the next action.\n\n**Measurement reliability.** State whether the indicator measures abundance, occupancy, area, survival, pressure or a subjective rating. Some indicators can move in opposite directions under the same ecological change. Identify which vital rate is most sensitive to feasible management before choosing a control action. A decision brief must still specify feasible alternatives, baseline and rights-related constraints; no quantitative success probability is implied.\n\n### Independent application exercise\n\n1. Sketch a causal graph of this case, naming a confounder and an observation that could disprove the leading explanation.\n2. Improve the survey design: specify sampling units, detection controls, an appropriate comparison and a revision trigger.\n3. Repeat the calculation with a 20% adverse change in one input, then draft a brief memo separating measured facts from management judgments.\n\n**Lesson takeaway.** Trace the scientific mechanism, observed indicator and authorized decision separately; no synthetic model is a forecast of a real site.",
