          "heading": "Lesson 2.5 \u2014 Metapopulations and rescue effects",
          "content": "**Guiding question.** How can we distinguish a defensible finding about **metapopulations and rescue effects** from a pattern that could arise through an alternative mechanism or a biased sample? The aim is to explain the ecological idea, define an appropriate quantity, reproduce an elementary numerical example, interrogate a counterargument, and connect the evidence to a legitimate practical decision.\n\n### The ecological mechanism and what it predicts\n\nOccupancy of habitat patches reflects colonization, local extinction, dispersal and changing patch quality; connected patches can reduce risk but may also transmit pathogens or synchronized disturbances. For this mechanism, identify the biological scale and time interval. Does an observation concern one organism, a local population or regional persistence? Name one observation that could contradict the explanation and distinguish the measured state from its inferred cause.\n\n### A field measurement contract\n\nMap patch identity, barriers, occupied status and repeated detections; analyze colonization and extinction separately. Define the sampling unit, eligibility, survey timing and denominators in advance. Keep auditable observations and appropriately restricted provenance so independent analysts can identify sources of bias.\n\n**Methodological lens \u2014 decision-focused study.** Specify the decision that will change if a result is high or low, then choose the least complex credible measurement. Collecting more data is not always useful if the result cannot change an authorized decision. Document how site choice or altered detection could change the measured result.\n\n### Synthetic ecological case and competing interpretation\n\nA fictional butterfly occupies five meadow fragments with one intermittently used stepping-stone corridor. This is a fictional case, not a measured local trend. Identify response, suspected driver and an unmeasured common cause. Would the conclusion change if the monitoring method became more sensitive?\n\n**Main interpretive trap.** Treating patch occupancy as a stable binary can hide seasonal dynamics or imperfect detection. Name a comparison site and independent indicator that could expose this error. Specify the result that would force revision of the preferred explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative case: Patch occupancy turnover\n\n**Assumed invented input.** Five of twenty initially occupied patches became unoccupied between two standardized survey years.\n\n**Expression:** `5/20*100`. **Calculated answer:** **25 % annual patch loss**.\n\nIdentify the units and denominator. This arithmetic alone supplies no uncertainty interval or causal evidence. Change a central input by 20%; report whether the management interpretation changes and why.\n\n### Evidence appraisal and practical choice\n\n**Inference and evidence.** Separate the estimand\u2014the quantity the study intends to learn\u2014from the statistic reported by a field method. A sample mean, proportion or index has a denominator and inclusion rule; a demographic forecast adds model assumptions; a management classification adds policy judgment. Ask whether the observation domain is representative of the population for which a recommendation is made. A confidence interval can capture sampling variation while omitting systematic selection bias. Apply this lens to **metapopulations and rescue effects**: identify the actual observation, the larger unsupported inference and the smallest useful independent check. Explain how new evidence might change the next action.\n\n**Comparator selection.** Choose untreated or reference sites using ecological similarity rather than ease of access. A valid comparator can differ from a desired restoration state but must represent a plausible alternative trajectory. Prioritize a network of suitable patches rather than only the largest surviving patch. A decision brief must still specify feasible alternatives, baseline and rights-related constraints; no quantitative success probability is implied.\n\n### Independent application exercise\n\n1. Sketch a causal graph of this case, naming a confounder and an observation that could disprove the leading explanation.\n2. Improve the survey design: specify sampling units, detection controls, an appropriate comparison and a revision trigger.\n3. Repeat the calculation with a 20% adverse change in one input, then draft a brief memo separating measured facts from management judgments.\n\n**Lesson takeaway.** Trace the scientific mechanism, observed indicator and authorized decision separately; no synthetic model is a forecast of a real site.",
