          "secNumber": "3.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 3.3 \u2014 Reserve design and representation",
          "content": "**Guiding question.** How can we distinguish a defensible finding about **reserve design and representation** from a pattern that could arise through an alternative mechanism or a biased sample? The aim is to explain the ecological idea, define an appropriate quantity, reproduce an elementary numerical example, interrogate a counterargument, and connect the evidence to a legitimate practical decision.\n\n### The ecological mechanism and what it predicts\n\nEfficient protected-area systems consider complementarity, habitat representation, persistence, connectivity, equitable management and leakage of threats to neighboring land. For this mechanism, identify the biological scale and time interval. Does an observation concern one organism, a local population or regional persistence? Name one observation that could contradict the explanation and distinguish the measured state from its inferred cause.\n\n### A field measurement contract\n\nRepresent habitat types and species requirements as coverage targets, account for costs and tenure, and test proposed portfolios under climate and budget changes. Define the sampling unit, eligibility, survey timing and denominators in advance. Keep auditable observations and appropriately restricted provenance so independent analysts can identify sources of bias.\n\n**Methodological lens \u2014 mechanism-based evidence.** Construct a directed causal diagram with the proposed driver, mediating process, ecological response and confounders. Association can be entirely real yet not caused by the proposed mechanism; test plausible alternatives. Document how site choice or altered detection could change the measured result.\n\n### Synthetic ecological case and competing interpretation\n\nA hypothetical reserve portfolio protects much mountain forest but leaves nearly all floodplain habitat unrepresented. This is a fictional case, not a measured local trend. Identify response, suspected driver and an unmeasured common cause. Would the conclusion change if the monitoring method became more sensitive?\n\n**Main interpretive trap.** Total protected hectares can conceal poor ecological representation or ineffective management. Name a comparison site and independent indicator that could expose this error. Specify the result that would force revision of the preferred explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative case: Representation gap\n\n**Assumed invented input.** Six hectares conserved against a thirty-hectare fictional target for a rare wetland habitat.\n\n**Expression:** `(30-6)/30*100`. **Calculated answer:** **80 % uncovered target**.\n\nIdentify the units and denominator. This arithmetic alone supplies no uncertainty interval or causal evidence. Change a central input by 20%; report whether the management interpretation changes and why.\n\n### Evidence appraisal and practical choice\n\n**Inference and evidence.** Separate the estimand\u2014the quantity the study intends to learn\u2014from the statistic reported by a field method. A sample mean, proportion or index has a denominator and inclusion rule; a demographic forecast adds model assumptions; a management classification adds policy judgment. Ask whether the observation domain is representative of the population for which a recommendation is made. A confidence interval can capture sampling variation while omitting systematic selection bias. Apply this lens to **reserve design and representation**: identify the actual observation, the larger unsupported inference and the smallest useful independent check. Explain how new evidence might change the next action.\n\n**Stakeholder assessment.** Identify who gains, who bears costs, who controls access and who can challenge a decision. Good biodiversity statistics do not supply consent or equitable governance. Optimize a connected, governed network with transparent targets rather than chasing area alone. A decision brief must still specify feasible alternatives, baseline and rights-related constraints; no quantitative success probability is implied.\n\n### Independent application exercise\n\n1. Sketch a causal graph of this case, naming a confounder and an observation that could disprove the leading explanation.\n2. Improve the survey design: specify sampling units, detection controls, an appropriate comparison and a revision trigger.\n3. Repeat the calculation with a 20% adverse change in one input, then draft a brief memo separating measured facts from management judgments.\n\n**Lesson takeaway.** Trace the scientific mechanism, observed indicator and authorized decision separately; no synthetic model is a forecast of a real site.",
