          "heading": "Lesson 3.5 \u2014 Climate refugia and shifting ranges",
          "content": "**Guiding question.** How can we distinguish a defensible finding about **climate refugia and shifting ranges** from a pattern that could arise through an alternative mechanism or a biased sample? The aim is to explain the ecological idea, define an appropriate quantity, reproduce an elementary numerical example, interrogate a counterargument, and connect the evidence to a legitimate practical decision.\n\n### The ecological mechanism and what it predicts\n\nConservation under climate change should consider microclimate, elevational and latitudinal movement, exposure, sensitivity, adaptive capacity and competing land uses. For this mechanism, identify the biological scale and time interval. Does an observation concern one organism, a local population or regional persistence? Name one observation that could contradict the explanation and distinguish the measured state from its inferred cause.\n\n### A field measurement contract\n\nPair regional projections with fine-scale temperature and moisture observations, dispersal constraints and scenario uncertainty. Define the sampling unit, eligibility, survey timing and denominators in advance. Keep auditable observations and appropriately restricted provenance so independent analysts can identify sources of bias.\n\n**Methodological lens \u2014 decision-focused study.** Specify the decision that will change if a result is high or low, then choose the least complex credible measurement. Collecting more data is not always useful if the result cannot change an authorized decision. Document how site choice or altered detection could change the measured result.\n\n### Synthetic ecological case and competing interpretation\n\nA montane plant retains a cool north-facing ravine although broad climate grids mark the entire valley unsuitable. This is a fictional case, not a measured local trend. Identify response, suspected driver and an unmeasured common cause. Would the conclusion change if the monitoring method became more sensitive?\n\n**Main interpretive trap.** One global climate scenario and one species-temperature threshold can miss extremes, snowpack and interactions. Name a comparison site and independent indicator that could expose this error. Specify the result that would force revision of the preferred explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative case: Temperature sensitivity contrast\n\n**Assumed invented input.** Microclimate sensor averages 23 and 26 \u00b0C; percent temperature differences lack thermodynamic meaning, used only as arithmetic illustration.\n\n**Expression:** `(26-23)/23*100`. **Calculated answer:** **13.043 % relative increase**.\n\nIdentify the units and denominator. This arithmetic alone supplies no uncertainty interval or causal evidence. Change a central input by 20%; report whether the management interpretation changes and why.\n\n### Evidence appraisal and practical choice\n\n**Research integrity.** Scientific disagreement is most useful when it is attached to a falsifiable assumption. Document the version of the habitat map, taxonomic name, protocol, instrument calibration and analysis rules used in the inference. If a relevant record must be generalized for species protection or cultural confidentiality, explain that limitation without revealing the restricted information. A transparent report can be reproducible without disclosing sensitive coordinates. Apply this lens to **climate refugia and shifting ranges**: identify the actual observation, the larger unsupported inference and the smallest useful independent check. Explain how new evidence might change the next action.\n\n**Measurement reliability.** State whether the indicator measures abundance, occupancy, area, survival, pressure or a subjective rating. Some indicators can move in opposite directions under the same ecological change. Protect plausible refugia and adaptation options without assuming historical ranges remain fixed. A decision brief must still specify feasible alternatives, baseline and rights-related constraints; no quantitative success probability is implied.\n\n### Independent application exercise\n\n1. Sketch a causal graph of this case, naming a confounder and an observation that could disprove the leading explanation.\n2. Improve the survey design: specify sampling units, detection controls, an appropriate comparison and a revision trigger.\n3. Repeat the calculation with a 20% adverse change in one input, then draft a brief memo separating measured facts from management judgments.\n\n**Lesson takeaway.** Trace the scientific mechanism, observed indicator and authorized decision separately; no synthetic model is a forecast of a real site.",
